// Book Two, Chapter 13 (session 24). The old brother at San Miniato, and the people the Lent nights are bringing to the door.
// A short opening scene (no phase), then one night phase of three actions out of four (San Miniato, the girl at night, Ser Bartolo's back office, the river stair), then the dawn.
// Ends with rest() and => next. Reads b2.answer, b2.berengaria_debt, b2.watcher_book, b2.sire, b2.fiammetta, b2.faction, b2.watcher_bond, b2.prisca_deal, b2.prisca_conspiracy, b2.paper and b2.loan.
// The book is incomplete, so play pauses after this chapter until chapter 14 is added.
// Later chapters read b2.anselm, b2.fiammetta_night, b2.medici_paper, b2.watcher_night and b2.prisca_promise.
@chapter b2c13
@title The Brother at San Miniato
@book blood-and-gold
@version 2

# start
## The weeks before the great feast
$ b2.anselm = "unseen"
$ b2.fiammetta_night = "unseen"
$ b2.medici_paper = "unseen"
$ b2.watcher_night = "unseen"
$ b2.prisca_promise = "hedged"
$ do chart("florence.bank")
$ do meet("anselm")
It is Lent, and the city has put on its gray. The stalls in the Mercato sell salt cod and dried peas, the bells go over the roofs a little flatter than they did, and in the evenings the confraternities walk the streets in their hoods with candles, singing something low. The bank has done very well out of it. Nobody has ever been so eager to settle his debts as a man who has been told that he may die before Easter.
?if b2.answer == "told"
  A boy comes to the side door at dusk, a small boy in a green ribbon who has been given a coin to run and has run a good deal farther than the coin was worth. He holds out a square of folded paper. It is in a hand as small and precise as a row of stitches. It says: "He is at San Miniato, in the cell under the stair. He was the one who painted the borders. He has not said a word since the page was scraped. I do not know what to ask him, and I have had nine hundred years to think of it. Perhaps you will."
?elif b2.answer == "traded"
  A boy comes to the side door at dusk, a small boy in a green ribbon who has been given a coin to run and has run a good deal farther than the coin was worth. He holds out a square of folded paper. It is in a hand as small and precise as a row of stitches. It says: "I will now name what you owe me, Signore. Go to San Miniato, to the cell under the stair, and find Brother Anselm, who painted the borders of the roll. Ask him one question, and only that one: what did you paint? Then come and tell me what he says. That is all. It is a small debt. I have been three hundred years finding someone who would pay it."
  You turn the paper over. There is nothing on the back. It is a very small debt, and you find that you do not trust it at all.
?else
  There is no message from the house with the blue door, and you did not expect one. It is the clerk who brings it, indirectly, on the river stair, with the little book open on a knee. There is a line near the end of the oldest pages, in the hand of someone who is long dead. It says that there is an illuminator at San Miniato who has not spoken in thirty years, and that no one has thought to ask why. "I have been carrying it for six weeks," says {watcher.they}. "I thought that it was a thing to be left alone. I find that I no longer think so."
?end
?if must_feed()
  ~> hungry
?end
$ do phase("night", 3)
-> night

# hungry
## Before the doors
The Lent has made the donors cautious and the streets thin, and your hunger has been patient for longer than it ought to have been. It comes back now all at once, very politely, like a person who has been waiting in the hall.
* Go to Benedetto's back room, where the cup is kept
  $ do feed("bank")
  It is warm and clean and nobody asks. You are very careful that no one sees you go.
* Take a sheep from a fold beyond the wall, and leave a coin on the gatepost
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ do earn(-1)
  It is a stupid, patient creature, and you leave the coin, and you are ashamed of the coin.
* Pay a donor from the Borgo
  $ do feed("donor")
  $ do earn(-4)
  $ humanity -= 1
  She does not talk, and you are grateful. The coin is warm in her hand when you go.
<~

# night
## Lent, after the bells
:night
?if actions_left() == 3
  Compline has gone, and the candles of a confraternity are going away down the Via dei Servi like a slow, quiet fire. Four doors in the city want you tonight, and you can go to three.
?elif actions_left() == 2
  It is the middle of the night. A fine cold wind has come down from the hills, and the lamps at the corners burn small and steady. You have the feeling, which has grown in you for a month, of a city that is holding its breath.
?else
  It is very late. Over the roofs of the Oltrarno the sky is still black, but there is a faint paleness in the east, which is only the hour of the cocks. There is time for one more door, if it is a short one.
?end
* Climb to San Miniato, to the cell under the stair [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> anselm
  $ do act()
* See to the girl, who has not been easy since the lane [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> girl
  $ do act()
* Go to the back office of Ser Bartolo, who has asked for you by name [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> bartolo
  $ do act()
* Go down to the river stair, where the clerk is waiting [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> clerk
  $ do act()
?if actions_left() <= 0
  $ do end_phase()
  -> dawn
?end
-> :night

# anselm
## The cell under the stair
$ do chart("florence.roadhome")
San Miniato stands on its hill above the river, white and green and very old, with the whole of the city laid out below it like a plan on a table. The monks are at their rest. You go up through the cypresses by a path that has been worn into the rock by a thousand years of shoes, and a lay brother with a lantern lets you in at a low door, looks at your face, and goes away again without a word, with the look of a man who has been told to expect somebody and has not been told who.
The cell is under the stair, as the paper said. It is no bigger than a cupboard. There is a narrow bed that has not been slept on, and a lectern, and on the lectern a leaf of vellum with a border begun, a small green vine and a gold bud, that has been begun so many times and left so long that the gold has gone to the color of a dead leaf. An old man sits on the floor with his back against the wall.
He is very old. It is not the face, which is mild and brown and rather vague, like a monk in the corner of a painting. It is the stillness. He has not moved in a long time, and he does not move now. His hands lie open in his lap. There is old yellow paint in the creases of the fingers. He looks at you, with a pair of blue and utterly patient eyes, and waits, as a man waits for a stranger to say a word that he has heard before and does not mind hearing again.
You have been told that no one has asked the right question. You have a notion of what a right question might be. You also have the notion that you may have only one.
:the_cell
* Kneel, so that your eyes are level with his, and ask him what he painted
  ~> asked
  -> :the_cell_end
* Sit down on the floor beside him, and say nothing at all, and wait for the bell
  ~> waited
  -> :the_cell_end
* Tell him that you know about the sixth panel, and that he is going to tell you what was on it
  ~> pressed
  -> :the_cell_end
:the_cell_end
<~

# asked
$ b2.anselm = "asked"
$ standing += 1
$ humanity += 1
$ bond.anselm += 3
$ do discover("the_roll")
He does not answer at once. His eyes go to the lectern, and to the leaf, and to his own open hands, and back to you. Then a thing happens in his face that you have seen before, once or twice, on a sleeper being woken, a slow, enormous, bewildered arrival, as of a person coming up a very long stair.
"Nobody has asked me that," says Brother Anselm.
It is a very dry, small, rusty voice. He clears his throat. He says it again, with more care, as a man tries a lock. "Nobody has ever asked me that. They ask me what I saw. I could not say what I saw. But I can say what I painted." He looks at his hands. "I painted a sun."
He tells it slowly, with pauses, in a Latin that has the sound of a very old and very careful school. The sixth panel was the largest. It was done in the days before the page was scraped, in the great hall of the old Senate in Ravenna, in white and gold, and the border was a vine. In the middle was a sun. It had six rays, which was an odd number, and he had asked why, and been told that it was the number of the lines. Five of the rays were done in gold. The sixth he was told to do in red lead, and he did it, and it is the only thing in his long life that he is quite sure was well made. "It was the best ray I ever did," says Anselm. "I have drawn it a thousand times since, in my head, and it is still the best."
He tells you the rest because he cannot stop now. The page was scraped by night, by a man in a gray coat, who did it himself with a knife, slowly, over many hours, and Anselm was made to watch. He says that the man wept the whole time. "He did not make a sound. He did not stop. He scraped, and he wept, and when he came to the red ray he stopped for a long while, and then he did it last of all." He turns his hands over, and looks at the paint in the creases. "I have wondered for six hundred years if he knew that I could see. I think that he did. I think that he wished that someone might."
He gets up, with an effort, and goes to the lectern. From under the leaf he takes a narrow strip of old vellum, gold and green and cracked, no longer than your hand. It is a scrap of the border. It was cut off the sixth panel before the scraping, by a boy who wished for a keepsake, and who was not seen again. "You may take it," says Anselm. "I have no further need of it. I have said it."
$ do obtain("sixth_border")
?if b2.berengaria_debt
  $ unflag b2.berengaria_debt
  You will have to take the rest of it to the house with the blue door, and it will not be an easy errand. It is, you think, the first debt that you have ever been glad to pay.
?end
You leave him sitting at the lectern with a brush in his hand. He is dipping it into a pot of gold that has been dry for thirty years, and the brush is moving.
<~

# waited
$ b2.anselm = "waited"
$ bond.anselm += 2
$ humanity += 1
You sit. It is a very odd thing to do, for a great vampire, on the stone floor of a cupboard, with your knees up like a boy's. He does not look at you, after the first moment. He looks at the lectern, and at the long unfinished vine, and the quiet in the little cell gathers and thickens, so that you begin to hear small things: the cypress branches on the wall outside, and the water in a gutter, and the lay brother's step going away and coming back.
It is a long wait. The bell of the monastery rings the hour of the night office, and the monks go by in the passage over your head, shuffling, murmuring, and the small sound of the psalm comes down through the stair like water through a roof. Anselm does not move until it has died away. Then he reaches into his sleeve and takes out a burned stick, and gets up with an effort, and goes to the whitewashed wall beside the lectern, and draws.
It is a circle. It is a sun, with rays, drawn with perfect and fluent ease. There are six of them. He draws five quickly, as a man writes his own name, and the last with much more care, and when he has done it he stands back. You see that the stick has gone dry in the last stroke and left a thin gray line, and he looks at it for a moment with an expression of the most open distress. Then he rubs his thumb in the soot, and goes over it, and it is red.
It is not red. It is soot. It is only the eye that makes it red.
He sits down again against the wall, and shuts his eyes, and does not say a word. You stay until the bell. You have learned a thing, and you are not certain what it is.
?if b2.berengaria_debt
  You do not have an answer that you can carry to the house with the blue door, only a drawing and a silence, and you are not certain that it will count. You are fairly certain that it will have to.
?end
<~

# pressed
$ b2.anselm = "pressed"
$ standing -= 1
$ bond.anselm -= 2
$ secrecy -= 1
It is the wrong thing to say. You know it as you say it, as a man knows that he has stepped off a stair. The mildness goes out of the old face as though a shutter had been pulled across it. He stares at you for a moment in perfect horror. He puts his two open hands over his mouth, quite slowly, as a child does, and presses them there.
You say it again, more gently. You say it a third time, less gently, and it is a mistake that you have made before. There is a step in the passage, and the lay brother is at the door with a lantern, and a second brother behind him with a cudgel, a stout and entirely mortal man, who stands as a mortal man stands when he has made up his mind to be killed rather than not to stand. "He is not well," says the lay brother, gruffly. "He is not to be troubled. You will go, if you please, Signore."
You go. The old man is rocking on the floor, with his hands over his mouth, and the leaf of vellum with its dead gold vine has fallen to the stone beside him. You have the feeling of having broken a thing that has taken a very long time to mend. You do not think that he will speak again, to anyone.
?if b2.berengaria_debt
  You will have to go to the house with the blue door and say that you were not able to ask. It will not be a pleasant visit.
?end
<~

