# girl_sired
## A very quiet hunger
She has been up since dusk. She is sitting on the end of the narrow bed in her wool wrapper with her hands pressed between her knees, and she has turned the lamp down so low that it is hardly a lamp at all, because she cannot yet bear the light of a flame. She looks up as you come in. Her eyes are quite dry, and they are not the color that they were, and she looks, in the dark, a good deal more like a Valerio than she ever did by day.
"I am very hungry," says Fiammetta. She says it as one makes a report. "I have been hungry since I woke. I have been sitting here for four hours telling myself that I am not to do anything about it until you came." She takes a long, careful breath, and lets it out, and does not need to. "I have found that I can hear the whole house. Benedetto's heart is going like a mill. I should like you to tell me what to do."
:the_girl
* Teach her yourself: take her to the back room, and show her how it is done, and how not to do it
  $ b2.fiammetta_night = "taught"
  $ bond.fiammetta += 3
  $ humanity += 1
  $ do feed("bank")
  It is not a lesson that you expected to give. You show her the cup, and the way of drinking from it, and then the other way, and the reason that the second must be rare. You show her how to stop. She is a very good pupil. She is white and shaking and absolutely exact. At the end of it she says "Thank you" in a very small voice, and then, in a different one, "Is it always like this?" and you tell her the truth, which is that it is, a little less each year, and that it is never quite nothing.
* Give her a purse and the key to the postern, and let her find it out herself, with one rule
  $ b2.fiammetta_night = "trusted"
  $ bond.fiammetta += 1
  $ secrecy -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  You tell her the rule, which is that she is not to kill, and that she is to be home before the first bird. You tell her where the donors are. She takes the purse, and looks at it, and at you, with a long, odd, level look in which there is a good deal of gratitude and a trace of something that is not. "You think that I can," she says. "I shall try to be worth it." She goes out by the postern in the dark, and you stand in the little room with the lamp turned down, and you do not sleep.
* Keep her in. Give her the cup, here, and tell her that she is not to go out until the spring
  $ b2.fiammetta_night = "forbade"
  $ bond.fiammetta -= 1
  $ secrecy += 2
  $ humanity -= 1
  She takes the cup. She does not argue. She drinks it down in the dark with her eyes shut, and when she has done she sets it on the floor with great care, and sits with her hands in her lap, and says, "I see." It is a very civil thing to say. She does not say anything more. When you go out you turn on the stair, and see that she has gone to the window, and is looking at the lane with her face against the bars.
<~

# girl_later
## A bargain in the dark
She is on the stair of the little room when you come up, with her chin on her knees. She has not been asleep. She has been here for some time, and she says at once, without preface, in the voice of a person who has rehearsed it, "I have counted the days. There are thirty one until Easter. I should like to know whether you meant spring, as the weather, or spring, as the calendar, because they are not at all the same."
You say that you do not know either.
"Then I shall plan," says Fiammetta, "for both." She has the look of a young woman who is very tired, and very determined, and who has not decided whether to be angry. "There is something that I should like you to do tonight. I should like you to tell me what it will be."
:the_girl_later
* Tell her, truthfully and in order, what it will cost, and what it will give, and what it will take away
  $ b2.fiammetta_night = "taught"
  $ bond.fiammetta += 3
  $ humanity += 1
  It takes the better part of an hour. You do not spare her anything. You tell her about the hunger, and the sun, and the long, odd, drifting solitude, and about the day when the people that she loves begin to grow old before her eyes. You tell her about a girl in a courtyard in Rome. She listens with her whole body, as a person listens in a storm, and at the end of it she is quiet for a long time. "Thank you," she says. "That is a great deal. I shall think about all of it." She smiles a little. "I think that I shall still ask again."
* Say nothing, and leave the door open, and let her ask whatever she likes
  $ b2.fiammetta_night = "trusted"
  $ bond.fiammetta += 1
  $ standing += 1
  She asks. It is not what you thought. She asks how you eat, and when you sleep, and what you do on feast days, and whether you have ever been in a church, and what you do when it is very hot. She asks a long list of small questions, and you answer them as truthfully as you can, and at the end you realize that she has been taking the measure of your whole life, a stitch at a time, quite quietly, and that she has not been asking about the Gift at all. It is rather the thing that it is built on.
* Tell her that it is not to be spoken of again until the spring, and that she is to leave it alone
  $ b2.fiammetta_night = "forbade"
  $ bond.fiammetta -= 2
  $ humanity -= 1
  She gets up. She is standing a step above you, and for the first time her face is exactly level with yours. "You will find, Signore," says Fiammetta, very softly, "that it is a great deal harder to leave a thing alone when it has been offered by somebody else." She does not explain. She goes up the stair, and her door shuts, not loudly, and you stand on the landing with a very cold, uneasy sense of having said the wrong word to the right person.
<~

# girl_refused
## The gate on the Fiesole road
She is not in the little room. It is the first thing that you know, because the bed is made, and the wrapper hangs on its peg, and the key to the postern is not on its nail. Benedetto is in the passage with a candle in his shaking hand, and a face the color of tallow. "She went out at the second hour," he says. "She said that she wanted air. I did not see. I thought that it was only the Lent."
It takes you less than an hour to find her. She is on the Fiesole road, a mile beyond the gate, standing in the dark under a cypress with her bundle at her feet, looking up the hill at the lights of a long, low, shuttered house that does not show one lamp. She does not turn when you come up. "I wanted to see it," says Fiammetta. "I have been told a good deal about it. I wanted to know if it was a real place." She is perfectly composed, and perfectly white. "It is a real place. There is a man at the gate who does not breathe. He has been watching me for a quarter of an hour. I think that he is waiting to see which way I am going to go."
:the_girl_refused
* Take her home by the hand, and tell her what that house is, and what is in it
  $ b2.fiammetta_night = "taught"
  $ bond.fiammetta += 3
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ humanity += 1
  You tell her all of it, as you walk, in the cold, under the stars. It does not seem to be the right time or the right place and it is the only one that you have. She listens to every word, her hand quite steady in yours, and when you come to the gate at the Prato she stops, and says, in a very small, very clear voice, "I am sorry. I was going to ask him. I should have gone, I think, if you had not come." You say that you know. It is the nearest that you have ever been to her.
* Ask her what she saw, and let her say it, and walk back at her side
  $ b2.fiammetta_night = "trusted"
  $ bond.fiammetta += 2
  $ standing += 1
  She has seen a good deal. She has the eye of her house, and she has been standing under the cypress for an hour. She tells you what lamps were lit, and which were not, and how many men were on the wall, and where the stable door is. She tells you, in a low voice, that there was a carriage with its curtains drawn, and that she is fairly sure she saw it at the Pazzi house on Sunday. You listen, and say nothing, and the Valerio chin goes up a little at every item, as it will in front of a person who is paying attention.
* Take her home, and lock her door, and tell her that if she goes again you will take her back to the hill
  $ b2.fiammetta_night = "forbade"
  $ bond.fiammetta -= 2
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ humanity -= 1
  She does not struggle. It is almost worse. She walks beside you the whole mile with her bundle in her arms, her face turned away, and she goes up the stair and into the little room, and you turn the key, and stand on the other side of the door with your forehead against the wood. After a long time you hear her sit down on the bed. You hear her say, very quietly, to the wall, "I shall ask him then." You are not certain that you were meant to hear it.
<~

# bartolo
## The Medici back office
$ do chart("florence.bank")
Ser Bartolo has not been in a hurry in the whole of the time that you have known him, and he is not in a hurry now. It is a good deal worse. He receives you in a small, bare, warm room behind the Medici counting house, among stacks of letters tied in red tape, with a single candle and a jug of the thin sour wine that is all that anyone in Florence will drink in Lent. He has been there for a very long time. He does not stand up. "I have a small difficulty," he says, with great politeness. "I should like to tell you what it is."
He tells it very quietly. The Medici bank had the papal accounts in Rome, which is the best business in Christendom. They had them until a month ago, when the Holy See transferred them, by a single letter, with a short and civil and unmistakable courtesy, to another house. It is not a thing that a bank can fight. It is a very large sum of money, and a very large amount of good name, and the other house is one that you know. It is not hard to see where the money is going. A good many people in Florence are taking a lively interest in what happens to a house that has lost the Pope.
"The paper that the Medici owe is good," says Bartolo, "and it will be good. But it will be a spring, I think, or a summer, before it is seen to be. I need a certain sum of coin for ninety days. It would be a great kindness. I do not offer an interest that you will think very fine." He turns the cup in his fingers. "I do not know anyone else to ask. It has been a very odd Lent."
:the_bartolo
$ _mp_n = 0
$ _mp_rank = 0
:mp_more_1
